---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="What are Content Credentials, and can they prove a photo is real?"
  description="A plain explanation of C2PA Content Credentials: what they record, what they genuinely prove, what they cannot prove, and why most images do not have them."
  path="/what-are-content-credentials"
>
  <section class="wrap doc">
    <h1>What Content Credentials actually prove</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Short version: they prove who signed a file and that it has not changed since. They do not
      prove the signer told the truth, and their absence proves nothing at all.
    </p>

    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>
      A Content Credential is a record attached to a file listing how it was made and what was done
      to it afterwards. The record is signed with a certificate, and the signature covers the pixels
      as well as the claims. Change one pixel and the signature stops matching.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard behind it is called C2PA, and it is backed by Adobe, Microsoft, Google, OpenAI,
      the BBC, Sony, Nikon and Leica among others. It is the closest thing there is to a shared
      answer to "where did this picture come from".
    </p>

    <h2>What it genuinely proves</h2>
    <ul class="points">
      <li>That a specific certificate holder signed this exact file.</li>
      <li>That the file has not been altered since that signature was applied.</li>
      <li>What that signer chose to record: the software used, the edits made, the source material.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it cannot prove</h2>
    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        That the claims are true. A credential is a signed statement. If you do not trust the signer,
        the statement is worth nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        That an image is real. Someone can photograph a convincing fake with a credential-signing
        camera, and the credential will faithfully record that a real camera took a real photograph
        of something staged.
      </li>
      <li>
        That an image without credentials is suspect. Most files have none, and stripping them is
        trivial and usually accidental.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why credentials disappear</h2>
    <p>
      Most social platforms re-encode uploads, which destroys the credential unless the platform
      deliberately carries it across. Screenshots lose it by definition. Many editors drop it on
      save. So a file that once had a credential very often arrives without one, through no fault of
      anybody.
    </p>

    <h2>How this compares to an AI detector</h2>
    <p>
      Tools claiming to detect AI images by looking at the pixels are guessing, and they are wrong
      often enough to be dangerous, in both directions. They label real photographs as synthetic and
      wave synthetic ones through.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading a credential is a different kind of act. It does not analyse the image at all. It
      checks a signature and reports what the signer wrote. That is a far narrower claim, and it is
      the reason it can be trusted where a detector cannot.
    </p>

    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href="/">Check an image</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

